    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "d8f8445a_57e60708",
        "filename": "kernel/sched/fair.c",
        "patchSetId": 2
      },
      "lineNbr": 3955,
      "author": {
        "id": 1456182
      },
      "writtenOn": "2021-12-17T17:33:05Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "I\u0027m not sure here. This doesn\u0027t seem right. With. uclamp_max, the problem was that when we had many tasks on the rq, then max-aggregation was a bit aggressive to allow good power savings.\n\nBut the scenario you describe here is that the task was boosted errneously and it feels a bit hacky for the kernel to handle this.\n\nShouldn\u0027t we improve the task classification so that only the important ones are tagged? This will help with prefer_idle being too heavy handed at cgroup level too where only a minority of the top-app tasks actually need it.\n\nIn the long run hopefully we can move to per-task tagging. But I understand until we get there we need to make the cgroup behavior better. Isn\u0027t there a way for framework to know if the tasks are foreground or background tasks? I\u0027ve seen in the last dev summit that Android API has such distinction which hopefully can help for a better classifications of which of the top-app tasks need to be boosted?\n\nhttps://www.youtube.com/watch?v\u003dspdNAwsC1GQ",
      "parentUuid": "bb91d31f_ee4231fa",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 3955,
        "startChar": 19,
        "endLine": 3955,
        "endChar": 47
      },
      "revId": "d356eedb7ba258936041277afed8e8507b6aa90f",
      "serverId": "85c56323-6fa9-3386-8a01-6480fb634889"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "04e0e250_0b35e525",
